Clear claims that are positively dead: our runtime, and the process
demonstrably absent. Never a claim that is live, unverifiable, or merely
unrecognised — the whole point of separating reap from check is that
clearing someone else's claim stays an explicit act.

An expired lease does not need reaping. It is already takeable, so claim
handles it directly and reap can ignore it.

Note .pidly/.id is now only a fallback for systems with no boot id, so the
identity work this ticket originally carried is done in 3.

pidly reap [n] — one ticket, or a sweep of the whole tracker including closed
work, since a dead claim on finished work is still something check reports.

Named, it says why it will not act: a live claim, an unclaimed ticket, an
unverifiable claim ("a claim you cannot verify is not a dead one"), or an
expired lease, which needs no clearing because claim already takes it. The
sweep skips those silently and counts what it cleared.

Each claim is re-read under the lock before it is cleared. The scan is not a
snapshot: between finding a dead claim and clearing it, the dead agent's
replacement may have taken the ticket, and that one is alive.

Locks were left out. A lock names a local pid and lock() breaks a dead one on
the next attempt, so a stale lock heals itself and reap has nothing to add.

Done: bin/pidly, covered by tests/reap.sh (24 cases, mostly the refusals).
